For basic values like a list of phone numbers:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {
    @Id @Column(name = "account_id") private String accountId;
    private String holderName;

    // List of basic values — no @Entity, no @Id needed
    @ElementCollection(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @CollectionTable(name = "account_phones",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "account_id"))
    @Column(name = "phone_number")
    @OrderColumn(name = "phone_order")  // preserves list order
    private List<String> phoneNumbers = new ArrayList<>();
}
// Creates table: account_phones (account_id FK, phone_number VARCHAR, phone_order INT)
// No @Entity for phone! No PhoneRepository! Just strings in a table.
```

For @Embeddable value objects (composite values without identity):

```java
@Embeddable
public record Address(String line1, String city, String state, String pincode) {}

@Entity
public class Customer {
    @Id private String customerId;

    @ElementCollection
    @CollectionTable(name = "customer_addresses",
        joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "customer_id"))
    private List<Address> addresses = new ArrayList<>();
}
// Creates table: customer_addresses (customer_id FK, line1, city, state, pincode)
// Address has no @Id — it's a value object, not an entity.
```

The DELETE-ALL + REINSERT problem:

```java
// ═══ THE EXPENSIVE UPDATE ═══
@Transactional
public void addPhone(String accountId, String newPhone) {
    Account account = accountRepo.findById(accountId).orElseThrow();
    account.getPhoneNumbers().add(newPhone);  // add ONE phone
}
// At flush, Hibernate generates:
// DELETE FROM account_phones WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'  ← delete ALL phones!
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone_number, phone_order) VALUES ('SAV2001', '9876543210', 0)
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone_number, phone_order) VALUES ('SAV2001', '9876543211', 1)
// INSERT INTO account_phones (account_id, phone_number, phone_order) VALUES ('SAV2001', '1234567890', 2) ← new
// WHY: elements have no @Id → Hibernate can't diff (which was added/removed?)
// So it nukes everything and re-inserts the whole list.
// For 3 phones: 1 DELETE + 3 INSERTs = OK.
// For 10,000 tags: 1 DELETE + 10,001 INSERTs = TERRIBLE!
```

When to use @ElementCollection vs @OneToMany:

```java
// @ElementCollection:
// (Recommended) Small collections (< 20 elements)
// (Recommended) Rarely changed (phone numbers, tags, roles)
// (Recommended) Simple values (String, Integer) or value objects (@Embeddable)
// (Recommended) No need for independent querying or cascading logic
// (Avoid) Large collections (hundreds/thousands of elements)
// (Avoid) Frequently mutated (add/remove often)
// (Avoid) Need to query individual elements efficiently

// @OneToMany (with proper @Entity child):
// (Recommended) Large collections
// (Recommended) Frequently mutated (individual elements have @Id → targeted UPDATE/DELETE)
// (Recommended) Need to query/filter individual elements
// (Recommended) Need cascading, lifecycle callbacks, versioning
```

